## Behaviour notes

- `notify` must be `false`. The provider manages Observe alerts only and never activates notify mode.
- `slug`, `severity`, {{ if ne .Name "o11y_slo_burn_alert" }}`scope_json`, {{ end }}and `recipe_config_json` replace the alert when they change, because the update API cannot change them.
{{- if eq .Name "o11y_slo_burn_alert" }}
- `recipe_config_json` holds `o11y_slo.<name>.current_revision_id`, so any SLO change that mints a revision also replaces this alert. Terraform archives the alert, updates the SLO, and creates a new alert with a new `id`. The provider cannot keep the existing alert, because it does not send a burn-alert disposition. See [o11y_slo](slo.md#behaviour-notes).
- When `recipe_config_json` refers to an `o11y_slo` created in the same apply, the value is unknown at plan time and the provider cannot check it. A read-only field such as `target_percent` then fails the apply, and the next plan refuses it.
{{- end }}
{{- if eq .Name "o11y_query_threshold_alert" }}
- `alert_class` is `outcome`, `budget`, or `symptom`. The server refuses `system`, which is reserved for platform alerts.
{{- end }}

## Import

```shell
terraform import {{.Name}}.example <alert definition id>
```

After import the JSON attributes hold the provider's canonical form: snake_case names, full enum names, 64-bit integers as JSON strings, and zero-valued fields left out. Unless your configuration matches that form, the first plan replaces the alert for a difference in {{ if ne .Name "o11y_slo_burn_alert" }}`scope_json` or {{ end }}`recipe_config_json`, and updates it in place for the other JSON attributes. Copy the values from `terraform state show`, or write these fields as strings:

- `evaluation_settings_json`: `interval_seconds`, `pending_for_seconds`, `recovering_for_seconds`, `preview_horizon_seconds`.
- `sample_guard_json`: `minimum_events`.
{{- if ne .Name "o11y_slo_burn_alert" }}
- `scope_json`: `typed_values[].int_value` and `typed_values[].uint_value`.
{{- end }}
{{- if eq .Name "o11y_agent_quality_alert" }}
- `recipe_config_json`: `short_window_seconds`, `long_window_seconds`, `baseline_window_seconds`, `min_run_count`.
{{- else if eq .Name "o11y_cost_per_success_alert" }}
- `recipe_config_json`: `window_seconds`, `min_success_count`.
{{- else if eq .Name "o11y_slo_burn_alert" }}
- `recipe_config_json`: `fast_short_window_seconds`, `fast_long_window_seconds`, `slow_short_window_seconds`, `slow_long_window_seconds`, `min_request_count`.
{{- else if eq .Name "o11y_advanced_signal_alert" }}
- `recipe_config_json`: `min_log_errors`, `min_tool_failures`, `min_fallback_count`.
{{- else }}
- `recipe_config_json`: `window_seconds`, `evaluation_delay_seconds`, `minimum_event_count`, `filters[].values[].integer_value`.
{{- end }}

The imported `evaluation_settings_json` also includes `interval_seconds` and `no_data_behavior`, and `owner_json` includes `display_name` and `active`. Add them, or accept one in-place update.
{{- else }}

